WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.036
-Our next guest is a six-time
Grammy Award-winning

00:00:04.036 --> 00:00:05.238
singer and songwriter.

00:00:05.238 --> 00:00:08.041
Her #1 "New York Times"
best-selling memoir,

00:00:08.041 --> 00:00:09.375
"Broken Horses,"

00:00:09.375 --> 00:00:11.110
is now available
as an audiobook.

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:13.245
Please welcome back to the show
Brandi Carlile.

00:00:13.245 --> 00:00:15.148
How are you, Brandi?

00:00:15.148 --> 00:00:16.883
-I'm good. How are you, Seth?

00:00:16.883 --> 00:00:18.251
-I am wonderful.

00:00:18.251 --> 00:00:22.355
It is very strange in these days
to Zoom with our guests,

00:00:22.355 --> 00:00:25.257
but it does provide guests to be
in very interesting places.

00:00:25.257 --> 00:00:28.628
And you are joining us,
is it true, from a fishing trip?

00:00:28.628 --> 00:00:31.197
-I am. I'm in a very remote
fishing village

00:00:31.197 --> 00:00:33.833
up in the peninsula
in Washington State.

00:00:33.833 --> 00:00:36.002
-And is this something
that you planned to do

00:00:36.002 --> 00:00:39.538
knowing it was
when your book was coming out?

00:00:39.538 --> 00:00:44.443
-I mean, I just celebrated my
#1 "New York Times" best-seller

00:00:44.443 --> 00:00:48.580
by staying in a $39 motel room
in a fishing village.

00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:51.217
Yeah. Check it out. Look.

00:00:51.217 --> 00:00:52.918
-Oh, it's beautiful.

00:00:52.918 --> 00:00:54.053
Oh, that's so --
[ Laughter ]

00:00:54.053 --> 00:00:57.456
You must --
You must get me the information.

00:00:57.456 --> 00:01:00.560
-And I got --
I'm all geared up to go.

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:03.363
-[ Laughs ]
It's so -- It's so rare

00:01:03.363 --> 00:01:05.898
that we talk to someone
who has a knife on their belt.

00:01:05.898 --> 00:01:08.601
-Yeah, I know. I just thought
you might like that.

00:01:08.601 --> 00:01:10.169
-Also, I think if you thought
we're going to have

00:01:10.169 --> 00:01:11.971
a "New York Times"
not just best-selling author,

00:01:11.971 --> 00:01:13.906
#1 best-selling author,

00:01:13.906 --> 00:01:16.509
I think if you thought that,
you might unfairly assume

00:01:16.509 --> 00:01:18.444
they wouldn't have
a knife with them.

00:01:18.444 --> 00:01:22.515
And I think that speaks to how
many different boxes you tick.

00:01:22.515 --> 00:01:24.150
-Well, thank you.
Thank you very much.

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:26.920
-[ Laughs ]
Now, I actually want to show --

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:30.757
We actually have a video of you,
the exact moment you found out

00:01:30.757 --> 00:01:33.526
you were #1 on the "New York
Times" best-seller list.

00:01:33.526 --> 00:01:35.027
Let's take a look.

00:01:35.027 --> 00:01:37.563
-You're the #1
"New York Times" best-seller.

00:01:37.563 --> 00:01:39.365
-No!

00:01:39.365 --> 00:01:41.033
One?
-[ Laughing ] Yeah.

00:01:41.033 --> 00:01:42.802
-Number one?
-Yes.

00:01:42.802 --> 00:01:44.069
-What?!
-[ Laughs ]

00:01:44.069 --> 00:01:45.905
No!

00:01:45.905 --> 00:01:47.973
[ Both laugh ]

00:01:47.973 --> 00:01:50.076
Oh [bleep]

00:01:50.076 --> 00:01:52.511
[ Both laugh ]
-Congratulations.

00:01:52.511 --> 00:01:53.813
It's a --
It's a really cool thing.

00:01:53.813 --> 00:01:55.648
And I have to ask, you know,

00:01:55.648 --> 00:01:57.217
when you write something
like this --

00:01:57.217 --> 00:01:59.485
And look, it's not like your
music isn't personal, either.

00:01:59.485 --> 00:02:02.255
But this is, you know --
This isn't metaphor.

00:02:02.255 --> 00:02:05.224
It's not, you know,
distant from who you are.

00:02:05.224 --> 00:02:06.459
It's very personal.

00:02:06.459 --> 00:02:09.128
How does it feel to, one,
share it with people,

00:02:09.128 --> 00:02:11.397
and then have it
so well-received?

00:02:11.397 --> 00:02:15.435
-Oh, I mean, it was scary then,
and it's scary now.

00:02:15.435 --> 00:02:17.736
And you're right.
That's the main difference.

00:02:17.736 --> 00:02:19.438
You can write these songs,
and you're shrouded

00:02:19.438 --> 00:02:22.175
in metaphor and analogy
and all these different things.

00:02:22.175 --> 00:02:23.876
And that's like --
It just doesn't --

00:02:23.876 --> 00:02:26.145
That's not what comes out
when you write a book.

00:02:26.145 --> 00:02:28.715
You know? I mean, I sat down
to start writing it,

00:02:28.715 --> 00:02:30.082
and about five weeks later,

00:02:30.082 --> 00:02:33.152
I realized it wasn't going
to fit into a 3 1/2 minute song.

00:02:33.152 --> 00:02:36.622
And, you know, I had to sort of
strip away all that metaphor.

00:02:36.622 --> 00:02:38.791
And it's -- It was --

00:02:38.791 --> 00:02:40.827
It's always going to be
a little scary, I think.

00:02:40.827 --> 00:02:43.695
It was well received
by -- by folks,

00:02:43.695 --> 00:02:45.198
which has made me feel --

00:02:45.198 --> 00:02:46.965
I just said "folks"
like the joke earlier,

00:02:46.965 --> 00:02:49.402
but hopefully everybody
understands what that means.

00:02:49.402 --> 00:02:51.904
[ Both laugh ]

00:02:51.904 --> 00:02:54.240
It's just that
it's life-affirming.

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:56.843
That's what it is.
It's life-affirming.

00:02:56.843 --> 00:02:59.078
-I wonder how it feels to --

00:02:59.078 --> 00:03:00.579
Because you recorded
the audiobook.

00:03:00.579 --> 00:03:02.348
So obviously, you write a book.

00:03:02.348 --> 00:03:04.317
And I'm sure you rewrite
and rewrite,

00:03:04.317 --> 00:03:06.785
and then it gets locked,
and it's a book.

00:03:06.785 --> 00:03:09.989
And then you have to go
and read it out loud.

00:03:09.989 --> 00:03:13.893
Did that -- Was that a other
totally different journey,

00:03:13.893 --> 00:03:15.427
doing that part?

00:03:15.427 --> 00:03:17.997
-It was fun doing it,
but I will never listen to it.

00:03:17.997 --> 00:03:20.166
I can't listen to the sound
of my own voice.

00:03:20.166 --> 00:03:21.834
And I don't know how you do it.
It's like --

00:03:21.834 --> 00:03:23.136
Doesn't it sound different
in your head

00:03:23.136 --> 00:03:24.871
than it sounds
when you say it out loud?

00:03:24.871 --> 00:03:27.173
-Yes. But I should note
that I'm like you.

00:03:27.173 --> 00:03:30.476
I don't particularly
like hearing it back.

00:03:30.476 --> 00:03:31.710
-Yeah.
-'Cause I think -- I feel like

00:03:31.710 --> 00:03:33.145
it's this very full --

00:03:33.145 --> 00:03:35.114
It's this very full
and strong voice.

00:03:35.114 --> 00:03:36.782
And then I watch, and I'm like,
"Whoa, it's really --

00:03:36.782 --> 00:03:38.117
It's very nasally."

00:03:38.117 --> 00:03:40.352
[ Both laugh ]

00:03:40.352 --> 00:03:42.054
-You do have a full,
strong voice.

00:03:42.054 --> 00:03:44.389
I actually quite like listening
to myself sing, though.

00:03:44.389 --> 00:03:46.192
-You do?
Well, that's nice to hear.

00:03:46.192 --> 00:03:47.827
Do you recommend --
Do you ever --

00:03:47.827 --> 00:03:49.495
When you're with a group
of friends, do you ever say,

00:03:49.495 --> 00:03:51.797
"Hey, we should
throw on some me"?

00:03:51.797 --> 00:03:53.098
-Yeah, I'm like,
"You know, why not me?

00:03:53.098 --> 00:03:54.333
Let's just --
Let's get in the car

00:03:54.333 --> 00:03:56.769
and just do me this road trip."
[ Both laugh ]

00:03:56.769 --> 00:03:58.304
-Do most of the people
you hang with,

00:03:58.304 --> 00:04:00.873
are they amenable
to that suggestion?

00:04:00.873 --> 00:04:02.141
-Well, they laugh at me.
-Right.

00:04:02.141 --> 00:04:03.242
-You know? Sure, you know.

00:04:03.242 --> 00:04:04.476
And I'm like, I have this --

00:04:04.476 --> 00:04:06.646
a healthy self-esteem
about my singing voice,

00:04:06.646 --> 00:04:09.248
and I like listening to me,
which is good,

00:04:09.248 --> 00:04:10.950
because no one else
in my family does.

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:12.919
-That's heartbreaking,
'cause you -- I know you have --

00:04:12.919 --> 00:04:14.454
You have two daughters.

00:04:14.454 --> 00:04:17.123
And they do not enjoy
listening to you.

00:04:17.123 --> 00:04:20.025
-One loves John Denver.
-Uh-huh.

00:04:20.025 --> 00:04:22.061
-And the other one loves
the "Frozen" soundtrack

00:04:22.061 --> 00:04:23.729
so kind of typical.
Really cliché.

00:04:23.729 --> 00:04:26.465
-Right.
-Really cliché kids.

00:04:26.465 --> 00:04:30.669
-You have a lot of --
And you speak to, I should say,

00:04:30.669 --> 00:04:35.608
a lot of your influences
in this book, from Elton John

00:04:35.608 --> 00:04:38.844
to Tanya Tucker to your mom.

00:04:38.844 --> 00:04:40.747
Did you --
Over the course of your career

00:04:40.747 --> 00:04:42.080
as you developed your own voice,

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:44.850
were you acutely aware
of how those influences

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:48.588
were finding their way
into your music?

00:04:48.588 --> 00:04:50.789
-I think like most young people,
I started out

00:04:50.789 --> 00:04:53.659
just mimicking
with a lot of affectation.

00:04:53.659 --> 00:04:56.228
And I'm not sure affectation
really falls away.

00:04:56.228 --> 00:04:58.397
I just think you diversify
who influences you

00:04:58.397 --> 00:05:00.433
to the point where
so many people influence you

00:05:00.433 --> 00:05:02.568
that it's hard to pinpoint
any one person.

00:05:02.568 --> 00:05:04.470
But, you know,
you can see it on --

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:08.807
in this audiobook,
where I recorded 31 songs.

00:05:08.807 --> 00:05:11.077
And like you said, they're,
like, wildly diverse.

00:05:11.077 --> 00:05:13.012
And all of these people
are my heroes

00:05:13.012 --> 00:05:15.615
and have been my heroes,
you know,

00:05:15.615 --> 00:05:18.117
in a more concentrated way
at different points in my life.

00:05:18.117 --> 00:05:20.486
And I think
during points of my life,

00:05:20.486 --> 00:05:21.753
I mimicked all those people.

00:05:21.753 --> 00:05:23.222
And now it's just mixing,

00:05:23.222 --> 00:05:25.357
a melting pot of it.

00:05:25.357 --> 00:05:27.693
-And now, of course,
due to your success,

00:05:27.693 --> 00:05:28.795
there are going to be singers,

00:05:28.795 --> 00:05:31.163
and there are singers
working right now

00:05:31.163 --> 00:05:34.867
who you can tell have been
influenced by Brandi Carlile.

00:05:34.867 --> 00:05:36.636
Do you have an ear for that,

00:05:36.636 --> 00:05:40.739
and is that something
that you are flattered by?

00:05:40.739 --> 00:05:42.541
-Yeah. I see it more,

00:05:42.541 --> 00:05:43.943
more and more as I get older.

00:05:43.943 --> 00:05:45.678
And I just --
I'm getting ready to turn 40.

00:05:45.678 --> 00:05:48.815
And I'm like "You know, I really
like that that's happening."

00:05:48.815 --> 00:05:51.150
'Cause I'm that kind of girl.
I do heroes.

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:53.019
I have heroes.
You know, that's how I am.

00:05:53.019 --> 00:05:56.889
And the thought that maybe
I could influence a young person

00:05:56.889 --> 00:05:59.625
or, you know,
be of service in that way,

00:05:59.625 --> 00:06:01.761
it's cool when I hear
a young girl sing,

00:06:01.761 --> 00:06:04.763
and she's got some nuance
that sounds like my voice,

00:06:04.763 --> 00:06:06.899
or she's wearing,
you know, a hat

00:06:06.899 --> 00:06:09.201
like the one I wear
every single day or something.

00:06:09.201 --> 00:06:11.637
I'm like, "Well, maybe
I'm actually changing things

00:06:11.637 --> 00:06:13.372
to a certain extent
a little, tiny bit."

00:06:13.372 --> 00:06:14.674
And I love it. And I hope so,

00:06:14.674 --> 00:06:18.578
'cause certainly,
a lot of people have changed me.

00:06:18.578 --> 00:06:22.181
-You talk about your mom
in the book a great deal.

00:06:22.181 --> 00:06:25.551
And you have a wild, wild story

00:06:25.551 --> 00:06:29.455
where for not
a short amount of time,

00:06:29.455 --> 00:06:33.959
she suspected that your dad
was a serial killer.

00:06:33.959 --> 00:06:36.128
-Oh, yeah, the Green River
Killer, actually.

00:06:36.128 --> 00:06:37.329
Yeah, where we grew up,

00:06:37.329 --> 00:06:39.231
the Green River Killer
was like the bogeyman,

00:06:39.231 --> 00:06:42.067
just destroyed
all these '80s childhoods,

00:06:42.067 --> 00:06:44.069
because he was just, like,
this awful thing

00:06:44.069 --> 00:06:45.471
that was in the news
all the time.

00:06:45.471 --> 00:06:47.439
And my dad and mom,

00:06:47.439 --> 00:06:50.109
they kind of just got married
right after they met

00:06:50.109 --> 00:06:52.845
'cause they --
They were pregnant with me.

00:06:52.845 --> 00:06:55.414
And so my mom
didn't know him that well.

00:06:55.414 --> 00:06:57.549
And he would always go fishing,

00:06:57.549 --> 00:06:59.085
you know,
at 4:00 in the morning,

00:06:59.085 --> 00:07:00.720
5:00 in the morning
to catch first light.

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:03.322
So, I'm, like, in my 20s,
years down the road,

00:07:03.322 --> 00:07:04.757
and I read a book about it,

00:07:04.757 --> 00:07:07.426
and I was like talking to my mom
like, "Man, Dad looked just like

00:07:07.426 --> 00:07:11.063
that Green River Killer fella
they got," you know.

00:07:11.063 --> 00:07:13.532
And she goes, "Do you want
to talk about this right now?"

00:07:13.532 --> 00:07:14.766
[ Laughter ]
I was like,

00:07:14.766 --> 00:07:16.302
"That's so creepy.
What are you talking about?"

00:07:16.302 --> 00:07:18.470
She's like, "For three years,

00:07:18.470 --> 00:07:20.038
I thought your father
was the Green River Killer.

00:07:20.038 --> 00:07:22.474
I almost went to the task force
and turned him in."

00:07:22.474 --> 00:07:24.277
I'm like,
"And you just continued

00:07:24.277 --> 00:07:26.545
to raise us in that house?"

00:07:26.545 --> 00:07:27.913
Yeah, that should
tell you everything

00:07:27.913 --> 00:07:30.950
about how eccentric and dark
and funny my --

00:07:30.950 --> 00:07:32.618
-It's so funny to think, like,

00:07:32.618 --> 00:07:35.521
"Well [Sighs] do I go
to the task force?

00:07:35.521 --> 00:07:37.356
It's just -- That's going
to be a whole thing."

00:07:37.356 --> 00:07:39.157
[ Laughter ]

00:07:39.157 --> 00:07:40.526
-I know.
"No, that's a whole thing.

00:07:40.526 --> 00:07:42.227
I'm just going to ride this out
and see how it goes."

00:07:42.227 --> 00:07:43.762
-"I'll just ride it,
see how it breaks."

00:07:43.762 --> 00:07:45.731
Hey, will you be out --

00:07:45.731 --> 00:07:48.501
I know it's -- You're like
three hours earlier than us.

00:07:48.501 --> 00:07:50.269
Are you done fishing
for the day?

00:07:50.269 --> 00:07:53.739
'Cause I know that's
an early, early thing to do.

00:07:53.739 --> 00:07:55.808
-No, I got out this morning,
and it was a little foggy.

00:07:55.808 --> 00:07:58.143
I couldn't see too far ahead
of me, so I just went locally.

00:07:58.143 --> 00:08:00.279
But today, I'm a go ahead
and motor out for a couple hours

00:08:00.279 --> 00:08:02.014
and go get after some lingcod.
-Alright.

00:08:02.014 --> 00:08:04.516
Well, I hope
they're biting for you.

00:08:04.516 --> 00:08:06.552
It's really lovely to see you,
as always.

00:08:06.552 --> 00:08:08.854
And do please come back soon.
-I will.

00:08:08.854 --> 00:08:10.789
It's lovely to see you, too.
Thanks for having me on.

00:08:10.789 --> 00:08:12.624
I'm so excited.
-Of course.

00:08:12.624 --> 00:08:15.361
"Broken Horses" is now available
as an audiobook.

